 # A soak is a count of cycles and a bound on what is alive
 
-<!-- TODO: one paragraph, in this project's voice, on what a soak is *here* and why it is not
-minutes: the same cycle thousands of times against one store, and the question `what is still
-there?`. Say why a wall-clock criterion would fail a correct step on a loaded machine and pass a
-leaking one on a quiet night, and point at the counters this project already keeps for work —
-[the phase profile](perf.md) — as the thing a soak does not replace. -->
+Every performance number in this project before this page is a **flow**: bytes written per payload
+byte, work per phase, blocks a plan did not read. Each one answers "how much did that cost", and
+none of them can answer the question a process that has been up for nine days raises — *what is
+still there?* A store can be fast and honest about every count it keeps and still hand a client one
+file descriptor per connection, and the first symptom of that is a machine that stops at the
+descriptor limit on day nine rather than a number anybody watches.
 
-[TODO](TODO)
+So a soak asks the other question, the same way `bench-budget` asks its own (see
+[the write-amplification record](write-amplification.md) for the shape and
+[the phase profile](perf.md) for the counters a regression is found in): run the *same cycle*
+thousands of times against **one store**, and read what is alive at the end of it. The harness is
+`tests/soak.rs`, the course's file; the counters it prints come from
+[`src/engine/soak.rs`](../src/engine/soak.rs) and, for the tasks, from
+[`src/service/soak.rs`](../src/service/soak.rs).
 
-## The cycle, and the five counters
+## The cycle, and the counters
 
-<!-- TODO: what one cycle is — ingest a batch through the protocol path, let the flush close it,
-let retention forget what is older than the window, ask one question about the span it just wrote —
-and why retention has to be part of it. Then a row per counter: what it counts, and what its growth
-would mean. `tests/soak.rs` prints them and `src/engine/soak.rs` answers them. -->
+One cycle is what a deployment does over and over: ingest a batch through the protocol path, let the
+flush close it into a segment, let retention forget what is older than the window, and ask one
+question about the span the cycle just wrote. Retention is not decoration — a store that keeps
+everything grows for ever, and the counters would say so, which is a different claim than a leak.
+
+Five counters are read after every cycle, and each one is a count of *state*. What its growth would
+mean is the whole of what the number is for:
+
+| counter | what it counts | what its growth would mean |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `open_tasks` | tasks the service has spawned and not reaped | a handle nobody let go of — one per connection, or one per maintenance tick, for the life of the process |
+| `open_handles` | files the store holds open right now | a descriptor held past the call that opened it: the machine stops at the limit long before the disk is full |
+| `store_files` | files the store itself names: the manifest, and the segment and the index of every ordinal the manifest lists | a store that never compacts and never expires, or a merge that leaves its inputs' names behind |
+| `store_bytes` | what those files cost together | the same claim in bytes: segments that are never merged stop being cheap to keep |
+| `records` | what the store holds, from the manifest alone | nothing at all — this is the counter that has to move, because a store that is doing no work has a steady state for the wrong reason |
+
+`records` is the control. The other four have to stay flat *while* it does real work, and a soak in
+which nothing moves at all is not evidence of anything.
+
+Two of the five are not the store's. `open_tasks` belongs to the service that spawned the connection
+and writer tasks — the ingest loop spawns them, and the maintenance ticks the harness runs are
+spawned through the same ledger — and it is *handed in* to the report as a number, because the
+engine runs in a test, in a repair tool and in a load generator and none of those has a task
+scheduler. `open_handles` is asked of the store's directory rather than of each half of it: the log
+and the segments of one store are one store, so they are one count, and two stores never share one.
+
+Two things are deliberately **not** counters here. The write path's log at the store's root is not:
+it is the buffer of records no flush has closed, this build never truncates it, and a run that
+counted it would be measuring the run's own length rather than the store's shape. And the files an
+*expired* ordinal's index leaves on the disk are not: nothing names that ordinal any more, so
+nothing can consult it, and [the layout](format.md) and `lakelet/4.14-index-pruning` say why it
+stays. A count of the disk would report that garbage as a leak once per expiry and teach a reader to
+ignore the counter.
 
 ## The steady state, and why the clock is not the judgement
 
-<!-- TODO: the rule the harness asserts — the same counters at the halfway mark and at the end —
-and the one counter that is allowed an allowance, with the reason. Then the cycles/hours
-distinction: the counted run CI does, and the long form (`LAKELET_SOAK_MINUTES=30`), which is the
-same question with a bigger sample. -->
+Every cycle writes a segment and retention forgets one, so a store that is working has the **same**
+counters at the halfway mark and at the end. That is the rule the harness asserts, and it fails with
+the sentence that names the counter which moved:
+
+```text
+the soak does not reach a steady state: `store_files` was 13 at the halfway mark and is 2013 at the
+end, which is 2000 more than the 0 this step allows it. A counter that moves between the halfway
+mark and the end is something the run opened and did not close
+```
+
+`store_bytes` is the one counter allowed an allowance, and it is a measured one: two segments hold
+the same records at two different times of day and the varints carrying those timestamps are not
+always the same width, so a segment may come out a few bytes longer than its predecessor. The
+harness allows a kilobyte — twenty times what a counted run has been seen to move and a fifth of one
+segment — and a leak moves this counter by a *segment*, not by a byte.
+
+Which is why the workload is the same records every cycle, moved on to the next window: identical
+work is what makes `store_bytes` a stock a run can compare with itself instead of a number with a
+guess attached.
+
+**And a soak is not minutes.** The command in CI runs a floor of 4000 cycles and bounds what is
+alive at the end; a machine that is twice as slow takes twice as long to print the same numbers. A
+wall-clock criterion would fail a correct step on a loaded machine and pass a leaking one that ran
+during a quiet night, which is why every `must` gate in this course is judged by counts. The clock
+is still *printed* — a reader wants to know what 4000 cycles cost — on a line the gate does not read.
 
 ## Running it
 
-<!-- TODO: the two commands, and the shape of the transcript: the five counters every tenth cycle,
-then the same five with `cycles=` and `refused=`. Say what each environment variable decides. -->
+The counted run is the project's own harness, and it is what the course's `soak` file drives:
+
+```sh
+cargo test -p lakelet --test soak -- --nocapture
+```
+
+Every tenth cycle prints the five counters — **one metric per line**, which is the shape every
+counter harness in this track prints (`tests/budget.rs`, `tests/attribution.rs`) — and the run's own
+totals come last, in the same shape:
+
+```text
+lakelet-soak open_tasks=2
+lakelet-soak open_handles=0
+lakelet-soak store_files=13
+lakelet-soak store_bytes=2741
+lakelet-soak records=384
+…
+lakelet-soak cycles=4000
+lakelet-soak refused=0
+```
+
+`LAKELET_SOAK_CYCLES` decides how many cycles the counted run takes; `refused` counts the cycles the
+store would not take, and a run that has to refuse is a run whose numbers mean something else.
+
+A real soak is the **same command with a longer workload**, and it prints the same shape:
+
+```sh
+LAKELET_SOAK_MINUTES=30 cargo test -p lakelet --test soak -- --nocapture
+```
+
+Thirty minutes of the same cycle is a bigger sample of one question and not a different question:
+the counters are read the same way, the steady-state rule is the same rule, and how many cycles a
+timed run gets is the machine's business. The harness says so out loud — its halfway mark is then
+half of the *clock*, because a run stopped by time has no cycle halfway mark to compare.
 
 ## The last time this ran
 
-<!-- TODO: the numbers from a run you did — the counters at the halfway mark and at the end, the
-wall clock, and which number moved and by how much. If nothing moved, say that and give the
-numbers; if something did, say what you did about it. A page quoting an intention rather than a run
-is the thing this step exists to prevent. -->
+Four thousand cycles, on the machine this page was written on, and nothing moved:
+
+```text
+lakelet-soak open_tasks=2      lakelet-soak open_tasks=2
+lakelet-soak open_handles=0    lakelet-soak open_handles=0
+lakelet-soak store_files=13    lakelet-soak store_files=13
+lakelet-soak store_bytes=2739  lakelet-soak store_bytes=2743
+lakelet-soak records=384       lakelet-soak records=384
+   cycle 2000                    cycle 4000
+
+soak: 4000 cycle(s) in 148s, 0 refused, 4000 frame(s) in the log
+```
+
+Thirteen files, 384 records, nothing open, two tasks — and the store did real work the whole time:
+4000 batches through the protocol path, 4000 segments closed and 4000 expired, 4000 questions
+answered. The one number that moved is `store_bytes`: 2739 to 2743, four bytes out of 2739, over a
+run whose whole steady range was 2734 to 2747. That is the timestamp varints and not the store, and
+it is what the allowance is sized against.
+
+The *first* run of this page did move a number, and it is worth saying which one and what was done
+about it: `store_files` grew by one per expired segment and the harness said so by name. The store
+was fine. The **counter** was wrong — it counted every `.lks` and `.lki` lying under the store
+directory rather than the files the manifest names, so the garbage an expiry leaves on purpose
+looked exactly like a leak. Naming what the store *names* is what makes the number flat, and it is
+the whole reason this page separates the store from the disk a paragraph above: a counter that
+counts the wrong thing turns a soak into an argument with the wrong file.
